[00:00] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: Creator introduces the topic of AI model effort levels.
AUDIO: 'Every single month we get a brand new set of models and with these set of models you always have to navigate the spectrum that\'s called effort and even with the newest of models let\'s take the GPT-5.6 family of Luna, Terra, and Soul. They have six sets of effort per tier of'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'model which gives you 18 possible combinations for any given task and naturally this becomes confusing. When do you use what kind of effort for what kind of task? And there\'s even a bigger issue which is a lot of people think'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that effort is a proxy for raw intelligence. So if you set your effort to ultra high, you must be getting the smartest model in the world. In reality, this isn\'t the case. So the goal of this'

[00:38] [scene:concept]
VISUAL: An illustration of a person playing a slot machine labeled "EFFORT" is shown.
AUDIO: 'video is very simple. I want to give you hyper clarity on what effort is, how it works, and how you can reverse engineer the best effort level for whatever model you\'re using even when nuance come out. And [music] near the end of this video'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to show you the nuances of using effort in your day-to-day work, I\'m going to show you one simple task that I executed across 12 different effort levels across two different providers. So if you want to focus your efforts in the right place, then let\'s dive in. All right, so'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'m going to break this down as simply as I possibly can. The way that people deal with effort is kind of like a slot machine where they will take a given task and just look at the names of the levels and assume maybe I\'ll just use high on this or extra high and medium'

[01:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sounds like it will do a worse job kind of confusing this effort level for a proxy of how smart the model will be on a particular task. So as new models come out, you start building a bad habit'

[01:29] [scene:concept]
VISUAL: A spectrum of effort levels (LOW, MEDIUM, HIGH, XHIGH, MAX) is displayed with associated token costs.
AUDIO: 'where you always assume you want to live on the right hand of the spectrum and just as our primary example here, I\'ll use Claude\'s effort levels even though I will touch on other model providers as well. You will live typically on this side of the spectrum and never even risk'

[01:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'using low or medium even though depending on the type of model, let\'s say it\'s a frontier model like a Fable 5, like a GPT-5.6 Soul, it might make sense to use the low level because the model leap itself in its model weights'

[01:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and training has advanced so far that it\'s much better than its lower competitor model right below it on extra high. Now, if you want the short summary of what effort level to use when, you can think of using low and medium on'

[02:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'everyday tasks, especially if you\'re using a frontier model. So, if you use Fable, I use Fable a lot on low effort mode because that is more than enough firepower to do the majority of tasks. The average model provider will set'

[02:21] [scene:concept]
VISUAL: The "XHIGH" and "MAX" effort levels are highlighted on the spectrum.
AUDIO: 'whatever language model you\'re using to high, which is a default zone. It\'s a decent zone, but even then, for the majority of work that I see people doing, you don\'t need this. This is still the equivalent of bringing a nuclear bomb to a fistfight. So, you'

[02:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'absolutely can steer clear of this for the majority of tasks. And if you don\'t believe me, like I said in the intro, I\'m going to show you a task I executed where the differences between the effort levels will be negligible. And when it comes to effort levels like extra high,'

[02:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'max, heavy, super deluxe, whatever the name is, these are effort levels that you rarely ever want to use because they\'re designed for extremely long-running tasks where there is nuance, where there\'s a need for'

[02:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reflection at every milestone of that task. And I can tell you from experience, from teaching entrepreneurs all the way to enterprises, that eight out of 10 tasks don\'t need this level of effort. And not only are these effort'

[03:10] [scene:concept]
VISUAL: An illustration of a multiple-choice exam with overthinking leading to the wrong answer is shown.
AUDIO: 'levels potentially not needed, but they can actually backfire on you. If you don\'t believe me, let\'s take a simple example. Imagine that you\'ve studied for an exam, and this is a multiple-choice exam with A, B, C, and D. And you\'ve gone through your answers so many times,'

[03:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they start to overthink what the answer is. You have so much of a thinking budget, even though you\'re pretty confident based on the simplicity of the question, that it\'s B. But you overthink to the point where now you select D.'

[03:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Many times, if you use extra high, max, ultra, whatever, unnecessarily, you\'ll end up using an effort level that thinks way too much about a very simplistic task. And like many people say, sometimes you want to hire the most'

[03:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'competent lazy person to come up with the path of least resistance. You might come into many scenarios where these different effort levels end up over-engineering a solution that could be way simpler. Now, my approach is to'

[04:01] [scene:concept]
VISUAL: A diagram showing Sonnet, Opus, and Fable 5 models with "LOW" effort dials.
AUDIO: 'be extremely intentional with first picking the right model, then within that model family, what effort level makes the most sense. And if we start from left to right, I will start with Sonnet on low or medium for the majority'

[04:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of tasks like changing the color of a UI button, changing the route that it goes when they click on it, Excel files, doc files, PowerPoint files. Anything that is generation-based can be executed by Sonnet. If I feel'

[04:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'like I need a much smarter model because I\'m dealing with a more complex problem, I want a deeper level of analysis, then Opus could make sense, but I\'ll start on low, and only if low is underwhelming me, will I go up in that chain. So, I\'m'

[04:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'very intentional about starting at the lowest level, then comparing the output to medium and high level. And once I understand the rhythm of that model, which honestly wouldn\'t take you more than an hour to understand, and if you don\'t want to spend that hour, I have'

[04:52] [scene:concept]
VISUAL: The "FABLE 5" model with a "LOW" effort dial is highlighted.
AUDIO: 'another trick I\'ll show you later on that you can use to expedite this, but once you settle on exactly what model to use when, then the effort level becomes much easier to use. And lastly, if I feel like I have a very complex problem'

[05:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'where it\'s worth the time, tokens, and money to use something like a frontier model like Fable, then I\'ll use it on low effort again, and I\'ll only climb that effort ladder if I have evidence that I need more firepower. Then before'

[05:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I break down each effort level and what it looks like one by one, I want to first talk about harnesses because the average person doesn\'t realize that whatever language model you choose is purely a brain in a jar, and that brain in a jar has no access to the outside'

[05:31] [scene:concept]
VISUAL: An illustration of a brain in a jar, representing the AI model.
AUDIO: 'world, the ability to manipulate your files. It\'s solely a model that has input text come in and output text, images, video come out. And by the way, if you enjoy the way I teach and break down these concepts, then you want to'

[05:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'check out the first thing down below for my early adopter\'s community. I drop a brand new module every single week in my Claude Code Living Course that\'s designed to either demystify something that\'s confusing &gt;&gt; [music]'

[05:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; or give you a way more leverage for the same input. So, if that sounds interesting, check it out and maybe I\'ll see you inside. All right, back to the video. So, whatever the frontier model is, it\'s always going to be a brain in a jar that needs its limbs to be able to edit your files, run the code, create'

[06:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'brand new files, spin up a local server, and actually see how it looks like. Which is why Google released a paper a few weeks ago that said that the importance of the model is only 10% of the process. 90% is the harness. And'

[06:20] [scene:concept]
VISUAL: A comparison chart of effort levels across four providers: Anthropic, OpenAI, XAI, and Google.
AUDIO: 'even if it\'s 80% or 70%, the majority of the magic that you see in these Claude codes, Codexes, is the harness itself. The model is what regulates what tool,'

[06:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'what limb should be activated for this specific task. And one important concept to keep in mind is that the same way that you can\'t have two models be exactly the same in behavior, you can\'t have effort levels mean the same thing across different providers. Each one'

[06:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'will behave very differently because it\'s effort based on how smart that model is in that model family. So, with Grok, you have only a few levels to pick from, whereas OpenAI is designing in a way that you have more control to pick'

[06:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'from different tiers of effort level. But you\'ll notice that depending on how well you prompt, plan, and spec whatever it is you\'re trying to build, the differences between each level is negligible. The most noticeable'

[07:11] [scene:concept]
VISUAL: A diagram illustrating the "Low" effort process: PROMPT -> THINKING (run 1 tool) -> ANSWER (~320 tokens).
AUDIO: 'differences you\'ll find are typically between low and medium and medium and high. Now, let\'s do a speed run through every effort level visually so you can see all the nuanced differences. So, if we take low for example, you could start off with a basic prompt and you would be'

[07:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'given a small thinking budget, meaning it will only want to use one or two tools, use them very quickly, and take the first result as the answer. And for tasks that are going from A to B, where like I said, you can predict all the'

[07:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tasks that are needed to be done and they\'re very straightforward tasks with little to no risk of error, this is more than fine. Now, when it comes to medium, you might have that exact same prompt, but it will use more of a token budget,'

[07:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and in this case, it\'s helpful because it might check its work and decide, "You know what? I took path A, path B might have made more sense." So, it will ruminate over its output and think about what is the best path it should have'

[08:00] [scene:concept]
VISUAL: A diagram illustrating the "Extra high" effort process: PROMPT -> THINKING (explore, test, retry) -> ANSWER (~20400 tokens).
AUDIO: 'taken, or whether the path it took was the best one. When it comes to high, this is where you start to see the most stark differences in token usage. And for tasks that need it, it\'s fantastic. It will sometimes force the model to'

[08:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'come up with a plan, then check the plan with you, then execute the plan, then double-check whether it executed it in the path of least resistance. And then we start entering what could be the dangerous territory, where you have the model plan, execute, but then over'

[08:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'evaluate all the possible options it should take, or it should have taken. So, instead of path A versus path B, you could see it go through path A, B, C, and D for what could be a pretty straightforward task. And if you want to'

[08:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'evaporate your entire token budget in one afternoon, possibly with one to two prompts, you would use max level, where it will plan, it will execute, it will think of many possible paths and many possible futures. You could get to a'

[08:52] [scene:concept]
VISUAL: A diagram illustrating the "Max" effort process: PROMPT -> THINKING (recheck, re-verify) -> ANSWER (~40800 tokens).
AUDIO: 'point where you\'re overflowing thinking. You are spending double, triple the token amount of medium for a slight improvement in the final output. Now, I\'ve walked you through this conceptually, but I want to show you an'

[09:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'actual task that I executed on Claude Code and Codex on every single effort level, and you\'ll see the results of all of them, and you\'ll be surprised at how similar they are. And after that, I\'ll give you a little mental model cheat'

[09:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sheet that you can use to navigate effort levels, no matter what new model comes out. If you take a look at the left-hand side, you\'ll see I have a variety of different terminal sessions, each of which running at a different effort level, and all of them were given'

[09:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the identical prompt. So, in this case, the prompt is the control group. I asked it to build a complete working project in a new folder called Model Pulse and to avoid it asking me clarifying'

[09:41] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: A terminal window showing the prompt for the "Model Pulse" project and agent execution.
AUDIO: 'questions, I told it don\'t use things like the ask user input tool or the equivalent in Codex. So, it was given this self-contained prompt and the whole point of it was to make a dashboard that could pull the latest posts from people on X to decide what is people\'s'

[09:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sentiment towards Grok, Codex models, Claude code on this particular day. So, it\'s basically like a stock sentiment tracker, but for model providers. And below this initial prompt, there\'s actually a lot more detail going into'

[10:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'what skills, MCPs, and APIs all of these models can access that have all my credentials, which is why I\'m not showing you. But, you can rest assured that all of them have been given enough instruction on exactly where to pull the'

[10:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'data, how to visualize it, and what my expectations are. And I ran this ahead of time so I can show you the nuanced differences between each level and tell you behaviorally where they made mistakes. So, starting off with Claude'

[10:31] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A web UI of "Model Pulse" dashboard for "CLAUDE HIGH" showing sentiment scores and a graph.
AUDIO: 'low, it put together this 30-day sentiment dashboard that doesn\'t have any points on it. Then it came up with these scores and at the bottom it has a series of posts that are meant to support each one of these scores. If you go from this one to medium, you\'ll see'

[10:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'at least this has some points. The scores look very similar. It has a better organized set of receipts, but the core nuance here is just the identification of the points on the dashboard itself. But, still not great.'

[10:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The next, if we go to high, we still have the points and now we have some nuance here where it\'s thinking of is the sentiment going up or down? Because before, it was kind of like this arbitrary number. You we don\'t know is 0.06 good or bad. Now, if we move from'

[11:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'high to extra high, you\'ll notice one core difference, which is it added a favicon. So, you want to ask yourself, is going from extra high to high worth the tokens to just be mindful enough to add this favicon? Then as you go down,'

[11:23] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A web UI of "Model Pulse" dashboard with "DAILY INTELLIGENCE / AI DISCOURSE" header.
