[12:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you could have accomplished on medium by just saying that you wanted it. In this case, you\'re just relying on the model spending and spinning on more tokens to get the same outcome. And if you go from high to extra high, the text looks a little bit different.'

[12:53] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A web UI showing "The posts behind the pulse" with positive and negative receipts for various models.
AUDIO: 'The dashboard is pretty much the same. This looks if anything worse than before. The receipts look worse than on high mode. And if we go and finish off with max, what\'s the core difference you notice'

[13:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'right away? Similar to max and high in Claude code, you get this favicon. So you want to ask yourself, this versus this little shadowing of the text, is this really worth double or triple the amount of tokens?'

[13:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And now we\'re showing the score out of one, so we know the denominator, which doesn\'t really add much. The posts look very similar back to medium level or high level. So, hopefully this shows you and gives you a pulse that if we gave it'

[13:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a better prompt that said, "This is exactly what I want." We didn\'t have to spend all the same tokens to get pretty much the exact same looking dashboard with some aesthetic differences across the board. Functionally, there\'s nothing'

[13:43] [scene:concept]
VISUAL: A decision tree diagram for selecting the appropriate effort level.
AUDIO: 'that is more novel, more insightful about one chart versus the other. It\'s all these slight nuances. So, spending tokens and way more time doesn\'t necessarily generate output. Now, ending off, I want to give you a framework of'

[13:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'how to think through using the right effort level at the right time. So, from left to right, if you know what done looks like, it\'s very clear the series of steps that need to happen, you can always start off with low, especially if it\'s a more frontier model. So, let\'s'

[14:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'take GPT-5.6, you would use sole on low effort and see how well that would do. Then, only if more usage is required or more bandwidth is required, would you go up to medium. And then you go up from'

[14:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'there. If you have normal daily work, then the default medium or high on a standard model or a high-level model is more than fine. Once you get into the areas of unknown unknowns, it might make sense to go into the highs. And if you have a very long-running task that'

[14:35] [scene:concept]
VISUAL: The decision tree diagram, highlighting the path to "XHIGH" effort.
AUDIO: 'really needs introspection through every single milestone that it goes through, then extra high might make sense once in a blue moon. But you want to make sure that depending on whatever plan you\'re on, that you can afford to run it.'

[14:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Because realistically, running multiple prompts saying, "Do this, check your work, consider that." On medium could eventually get you to the exact same output that you could have one-shotted with extra high. And an extra 20 minutes'

[15:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of your time isn\'t worth 100,000, a million tokens to spend on the exact same outcome. And when it comes to max, heavy, ultra, deluxe, whatever the name is, you should rarely ever be using it. Because like you would have seen with'

[15:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that one example, I could show you five more where you will get an incremental change between each effort level, but that incremental change doesn\'t justify the 4x, the 5x multiple of tokens that you\'re spending on that task. So hopefully this gives you a roadmap on'

